course: dictionary
lesson: 16077
title: Vocabulary in context: walkover
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word you do not know can often be worked out from the words around it. These words are its context, and they leave clues. «Our team scored six goals in ten minutes, and the other side hardly touched the ball.» Scoring six goals and hardly touching the ball are clues that the win was easy. So a walkover must mean a very easy win.
explain: Sometimes there is no game at all. «The other team did not turn up, so we won by a walkover.» The win is given because the other side could not play. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? An easy win fits, so that is the meaning.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «We won the match with ease» still makes sense, so walkover means an easy win. Now try «It was no walkover». The word no tells you the contest was hard, not easy.
fact: Walkover is a noun: one walkover, two walkovers.
fact: «Walkover» means a very easy win, where the other side hardly fights back.
fact: In sport, a walkover can also be a win given because the other side did not turn up or could not play.
fact: The phrase «no walkover» means the contest was hard and not easy to win.
fact: You will see walkover in sport, games, competitions and election news.
exercise: «Our team scored six goals and the other side hardly touched the ball.» What kind of game was this? | *A walkover | A draw | A penalty shootout | A close match
exercise: «The other team did not turn up, so we won by a walkover.» Why did we win? | *The other team could not play or did not come | We scored in extra time | It rained all day | We were the best players in the world
exercise: Mia says, «It was no walkover.» What does she mean? | *The game was hard and not easy to win | The game was cancelled | The game was very easy to win | The game was played in a field
test: «The candidate won the election by a walkover, as no one else stood against her.» What does walkover mean here? | *An easy win with hardly any fight | Lost badly | The election was cancelled | A tie with the other candidate
test: Which phrase shows that a contest was NOT easy? | A walkover win | Win by a walkover | *No walkover | It was a walkover
exam: Which sentence uses walkover correctly? | *Sam finished the quiz in five minutes, and it was a walkover | The team lost the match and it was a walkover | We walkover the bridge after school | The rain was a walkover, so we stayed inside
